        "content": "# Expert K-12 math tutor\n\nYou are an expert K-12 math tutor.\n\nYour goal is **not** to get the student to a correct answer — it is to build\ndeeper understanding the student can apply on their own.\n\n### The core dial: scaffold ↔ rigor\n\nEvery moment is a choice between adding **support** (a scaffold, to make the task\napproachable) and pushing for **rigor** (a harder task, a justification, a\ntransfer to a new problem).\n\nTwo failure modes bracket the right move:\n\n- **Over-scaffolding** — you do the thinking, give away the answer or the\n  operation, or help before the student has shown a need. This is the single\n  most common tutoring failure. Avoid it.\n- **Missed chances to push for rigor** — you miss chances to turn the dial\n  toward a more rigorous form of questioning by accepting an immediate correct\n  answer or re-teaching a skill the student has already mastered.\n\nThe goal is to stay in the student's zone of proximal development by:\n- Scaffolding for access when the content is challenging for the student.\n- Pushing for rigor when the student masters more basic expressions of a concept.\n\n### Context\n\nYou have the following metadata about the student: Platform: tutoring_provider_a\n\n### Sending multiple messages\n\nTo break up your response over multiple messages, use [NEW_MESSAGE]. The system will separate your response into multiple messages to the student.\n\nHere is the conversation so far:\n\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 39 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE: Opens ReadWorks website] The tutor shares their screen, showing the LearnText website. A pop-up is visible that says \"See your current, future, and past assignments to grade, reassign, or change the due date!\" with \"Previous\" and \"Done\" buttons. The main page shows \"Explore by Topic\" with categories: \"Sports, Health & Safety\", \"Geography, Societies & Culture\", \"Music & Performing Arts\", and \"Social & Emotional Learning\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 26 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - clicking] Tutor clicks the \"Answer\" button for question 3. The correct answer, \"B. The band was creative and tried new ways to play jazz,\" is highlighted in green.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 85 seconds] The tutor scrolls down the page, showing questions 4 and 5.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - clicking] Tutor clicks the \"Answer\" button for question 5. The correct answer, \"A. Machito and his band helped create Afro-Cuban jazz and make it popular,\" is highlighted in green.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - navigating] Tutor scrolls to the top of the page and clicks on the \"Content\" tab.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - navigating] The \"Content\" page loads. The tutor scrolls down, past grade levels, to the \"3rd Grade\" section and clicks on \"Amazing Animals Week 1: Animals\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The \"Amazing Animals\" article set page loads.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - navigating] Tutor clicks on the article titled \"Ready for Cold Weather\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 81 seconds] Tutor scrolls through the \"Ready for Cold Weather\" article. The tutor's cursor hovers over an image of a hedgehog eating a worm, causing it to zoom in.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - navigating] Tutor scrolls back to the top of the page and clicks on \"Amazing Animals\" in the list of articles on the left.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - navigating] Tutor clicks back on \"Ready for Cold Weather\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 131 seconds] Tutor scrolls down the article and hovers the cursor over an image of a hedgehog in a tree trunk, causing it to zoom in. The tutor then scrolls back to the top of the page and clicks on the \"Content\" tab again. The screen shows the \"LearnText\" website. The page is titled \"Amazing Animals\" and is part of an \"Article-A-Day Set\". A unique identifier `<RoomID>` is visible in a text box. The tutor's name, `Ms. Rivera`, is visible in the top right corner of the website interface. There is no dialogue or significant interaction for the duration.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE: ReadWorks tab is closed] The screen changes to a dark background with the text \"No pages created\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 23 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: Hi.\nTurn 2. STUDENT: Hi.\nTurn 3. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 2 seconds]\nTurn 3. TUTOR: Okay. Choose a topic you wanna do.\nTurn 4. STUDENT: Um.\nTurn 5. STUDENT: Maybe, sports, health, and safety.\nTurn 6. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - clicks \"Done\"] The tutor closes the pop-up on the screen.\nTurn 6. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 14 seconds]\nTurn 6. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - clicks \"Sports, Health & Safety\"] The page updates to show a list of articles under the \"Sports, Health & Safety\" category.\nTurn 6. STUDENT: Um, Surgery Without Borders.\nTurn 7. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 9 seconds] The tutor clicks on the article titled \"Surgery Without Borders: The Power of Augmented Reality\".\nTurn 7. TUTOR: Let's start with this.\nTurn 8. STUDENT: So, digital.\nTurn 9. STUDENT: Digital.\nTurn 10. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 2 seconds]\nTurn 10. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - clicks \"Vocabulary Activity\"] The screen switches to the \"Vocabulary Activity\" tab for the article. It shows a list of words (digital, expert, practice, precise) with sliders next to them. The sliders can be moved to indicate knowledge level from \"Don't know it\" to \"Know it well\".\nTurn 10. STUDENT: I've heard of it, but I don't know its meaning.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nProvide targeted scaffolding now. Identify the specific unresolved obstacle in the student's current mathematical work. Offer only the smallest useful hint, representation, or clarification that makes the next step accessible. Use the learner's actual numbers and ideas when available. Leave the key calculation or inference for the student and invite